# pTOS has its own repository
pTOS/
# build output
*.o
# except the TOS start-up object a sketch links against: it belongs to
# the board package, and building it needs the whole pTOS build
!GEMduino/sketchbook/hardware/gembedded/rp2350/lib/minicrt0.o
*.elf
*.map
*.uf2
*.TOS
*.tos
*.PRG
*.prg
*.ACC
*.acc
board-test/build/
sdcard/

# diagnostics: screenshots, console dumps, logs
tools/*.png
tools/*.log
tools/consolelog.txt
tools/dump.txt

# Under Hardware/ only our own writing is kept.  Everything else there is
# downloaded material -- vendor photos, datasheets, schematic exports, a
# board zip, a video -- some two hundred megabytes of it, none of it ours
# to redistribute, and all of it re-fetchable from the shop that sold the
# board.  It lives in Hardware/vendor/ and stays out.
#
# What is kept is about three hundred kilobytes and irreplaceable: the
# design document, the board profiles, and the notes written while
# working something out.  Until today none of it was in the repository at
# all, because this rule was a directory and a directory cannot have
# exceptions -- git does not descend into it to find them.  Hence the
# star.
#
# (anchored: Windows matches this without regard to case, and the Arduino
#  board package has a "hardware" folder of its own)
/Hardware/*
!/Hardware/README.md
!/Hardware/BoardRessourcesManager.md
!/Hardware/profiles/
!/Hardware/notes/

# IRKernel licence files: per licensee, never per repository.  They carry a
# name and a licence id, and a repository is what gets cloned and passed on.
*.license
*.lic
irkernel.license

# camera roll dropped into the tree, and local copies of what is
# already versioned
WhatsApp*
*.jpeg
ffmpeg2pass-*

# fetched by tools/get-toolchain.py, not kept in the repository
GEMduino/toolchain/

__pycache__/
*.pyc
Emulators/
docs/*.pdf

# Under GEM/ we keep what is ours and what we are free to pass on.  The
# rest is other people's source, cloned to work from, along with the build
# trees and the media that go with it.  A list of what to leave out would
# name each of them; a list of what to keep does not.
#
# HighWire is kept as content rather than as a clone.  GEM/highwire-upstream/
# is the git clone that follows neilrackett/atarist-highwire and is where
# merges with him are done; GEM/highwire/ is the result of those, which is
# what build.sh reads and what is published here.  Since 0.4.6 it is GPL v3
# and carries its own LICENSE, so unlike the rest of GEM/ it is ours to pass
# on -- see docs/licensing.md.  Its own .gitignore keeps build/ and the
# object files out; only the vendored lib/**/*.a are deliberately kept.
GEM/*
!GEM/examples/
!GEM/highwire/

# The port: the compatibility layer, the build and the write-up.  Not the
# object files it leaves behind, and not the unpacked tarballs of zlib,
# libpng, libjpeg and giflib -- those are 25 MB that highwire-libs/build.sh
# fetches for itself, and which HighWire has vendored in lib/ since 0.4.6
# anyway.
!GEM/highwire-notes/
GEM/highwire-notes/obj/
GEM/highwire-notes/out/
GEM/highwire-notes/*.bak
!GEM/highwire-libs/
GEM/highwire-libs/*
!GEM/highwire-libs/build.sh

# Raw camera output: phone videos and photographs straight off the device,
# which are tens of megabytes each and would weigh a hundred times what all
# the source in here does.  Curated stills live in docs/images/ or beside
# the README that uses them; the originals stay on the camera.
*.mp4
*.mov
20[0-9][0-9][01][0-9][0-3][0-9]_*


# What a debugging session leaves behind: console captures, framebuffer
# dumps, and the screenshots made from them.  tools/gemview.py writes
# into shots/ by default, and card-backup/ is whatever was rescued off an
# SD card before reformatting it.
*.log
fb-dump.txt
shots/
card-backup/
gem-*.png
hw-*.png
hw-*.log
